# Annotations for COCOSID.BIN. Regenerate the listing with:
#   python3 tools/dis6809.py -a cocosid.ann files/COCOSID.BIN > cocosid.asm
# Directive reference: the Annotations class in tools/dis6809.py.

# ------------------------------------------------------------- options
dp 48
dp-at 494C-4983 49
entry 49F7 49F9 49FB 49FD 49FF 4A01 4A03 4A05 4A07
ptrs 4B7D-4BAC
words 532D-542C
ptrs 553B-55A0

# ------------------------------------------------------------- header
header CoCoSID: a three-voice, SID-style music player for the TRS-80 Color
header Computer. All sound is made in software: three wavetable oscillators
header are summed and written to the 6-bit DAC at $FF20. Interrupts stay off;
header all timing comes from instruction cycle counts.
header
header Timing (0.895 MHz CPU):
header   sample      77 cycles, so about 11.6 kHz
header   mixer pass  227 samples, about 20 ms; one envelope frame
header   tick        one sequencer step plus three mixer passes, about 60 ms
header
header Song data:
header   song list   one per voice, one pattern number per byte. 0 stops the
header               voice, $FF restarts the whole song.
header   pattern     a stream of commands; see CMDTABLE.
header   instrument  15 frames of 4 bytes, built in RAM at INSTRS by command 2:
header                 +0  pointer to the middle of a 256-byte wave table
header                 +2  pitch offset in notes, times 2
header                 +3  unused
header               Frames 0-2 are the attack, 3-11 the sustain (looped while
header               the note is held), 12-14 the release. Volume envelopes
header               come from switching to half or quarter amplitude waves.

# ------------------------------------------------------------- symbols
equ V_NOTE   0  ; note number times 2
equ V_INSTR  1  ; instrument now sounding (2 bytes)
equ V_IBASE  3  ; instrument selected by the pattern (2 bytes)
equ V_FRAME  5  ; envelope frame, times 4
equ V_PATPTR 6  ; next pattern command, 0 = fetch a pattern (2 bytes)
equ V_SONGPTR 8 ; next song list entry (2 bytes)
equ V_DUR    10 ; ticks left before the note releases
equ V_GAP    11 ; ticks of rest left
equ V_GAPLEN 12 ; rest after the last note, 0 = tied to the next
equ V_BEND   13 ; pitch bend added to the frequency, voice 1 only (2 bytes)
equ V_BRATE  15 ; added to V_BEND every mixer pass (2 bytes)
equ V_SIZE   18 ; size of a voice record

field U 1 V_INSTR
field U 3 V_IBASE
field U 5 V_FRAME
field U 6 V_PATPTR
field U 8 V_SONGPTR
field U A V_DUR
field U B V_GAP
field U C V_GAPLEN
field U D V_BEND
field U F V_BRATE

# RAM outside the loaded image
label 4815 VOICE1
label 4827 VOICE2
label 4839 VOICE3
label 4BAD INSTRS
comment 4815 voice records, V_SIZE bytes each (RAM)
comment 4BAD instruments, 60 bytes each (RAM)
label 6395 WNOISE
label 6E95 WOFF
label 6F95 WSINE2
label 7095 WSINE4
label 7195 WSAW2
label 7295 WSAW4
label 7395 WTRI2
label 7495 WTRI4
label 7595 WPULSE
label 7695 WPULSE2
label 7795 WPULSE4
label 7895 WPWM
label 7995 WPWM2
label 7A95 WPWM4
label 7B95 WEND
comment 6E95 wave tables built by MAKEWAVES (RAM)
comment 7B95 end of the wave tables

# operand bytes patched while running
label 4955 V1_PHASE
label 4958 V1_FREQ
label 4961 V2_PHASE
label 4964 V2_FREQ
label 496D V3_PHASE
label 4970 V3_FREQ
label 4977 V1_SAMPLE
label 4979 V2_SAMPLE

# wave pointers (each aims at the middle of its table)
expr 6F15 WOFF+$80
expr 6C15 WSINE+$80
expr 6D15 WSAW+$80
expr 6E15 WTRI+$80
expr 7615 WPULSE+$80
expr 7015 WSINE2+$80
expr 7215 WSAW2+$80
expr 7415 WTRI2+$80
expr 7715 WPULSE2+$80
expr 7115 WSINE4+$80
expr 7315 WSAW4+$80
expr 7515 WTRI4+$80
expr 7815 WPULSE4+$80
expr 7915 WPWM+$80
expr 7A15 WPWM2+$80
expr 7B15 WPWM4+$80
expr 6415 WNOISE+$080
expr 6515 WNOISE+$180
expr 6615 WNOISE+$280
expr 6715 WNOISE+$380
expr 6815 WNOISE+$480
expr 6915 WNOISE+$580
expr 6A15 WNOISE+$680
expr 6B15 WNOISE+$780

# ------------------------------------------------------------- globals
block 4800 Entry point and globals. DP is $48 while the player runs, so these
block 4800 and the voice records are reached with direct-page addressing.
label 4803 CMD2COUNT
label 4806 DISPATCH
label 480E SAMPCOUNT
label 480F PWMDIR
label 4810 PWMPOS
label 4811 TICKS
label 4812 SAVED_S
comment 4803 frame counter for command 2; $4804-$4805 unused
comment 4806 command handler address (2 bytes); $4808-$480D unused
comment 480E samples left in this mixer pass
comment 480F PWM sweep direction: 0 = grow high part
comment 4810 PWM edge position within WPWM
comment 4811 tick counter, never read
comment 4812 S saved by MIXER (2 bytes); $4814 unused

# ------------------------------------------------------------- startup
label 484B HOOK
block 484B HOOK: called once at startup; just RTS. The next three bytes are
block 484B also RTS and are never reached.

label 484F INIT
block 484F INIT: take over the machine, build the wave tables, then play.
comment 484F player variables live in page $48
comment 4853 mask IRQ and FIRQ
comment 4855 stack below $8000
comment 4859 disable the HSYNC interrupt
comment 4861 disable the VSYNC interrupt
comment 4869 drive motors off
comment 486C startup hook (just RTS)
comment 486E build all wave tables

label 4871 RESTART
block 4871 RESTART: also entered from the sequencer when voice 1's song list
block 4871 reaches $FF, possibly from inside a subroutine, so the stack is reset.
comment 4875 set up the DAC and rewind every voice

label 4879 SNDINIT
block 4879 SNDINIT: enable sound output, then point each voice at the start of
block 4879 its song list with its counters cleared.
comment 487E PIA1 CB2 high: sound enable
comment 4886 PIA1 port A: select the DDR
comment 4889 dummy read
comment 488E bits 2-7 (DAC) out, bits 0-1 in
comment 4893 back to the data register
comment 4896 voice 1 song list
comment 489B voice 2 song list
comment 48A0 voice 3 song list
comment 48A5 clear durations, rests, patterns and bend
comment 48C5 rest length 1, so the first note
comment 48C7 starts its envelope from frame 0
comment 48CD all voices start on instrument 0

# ------------------------------------------------------------- main loop
label 48D7 MAINLOOP
block 48D7 MAINLOOP: one tick. The sequencer runs once and the mixer three
block 48D7 times, with a PWM step between mixer passes. Never returns.
comment 48E4 count ticks (never read)

# ------------------------------------------------------------- mixer
label 48EA MIXER
block 48EA MIXER: make 227 samples (one envelope frame) from all three voices.
block 48EA For each voice: read the current instrument frame (wave pointer and
block 48EA pitch offset), step to the next frame, look up the frequency and
block 48EA patch it into the sample loop. Voice 1 also applies its pitch bend.
block 48EA Y, U and S hold the three wave pointers, so S is saved first.
comment 48EA S becomes voice 3's wave pointer
comment 48ED voice 1: X = instrument frame
comment 48F3 Y = voice 1 wave
comment 48F6 B = frame's pitch offset
comment 48F8 next frame on the next pass
comment 48FC frequency of note + offset
comment 4904 plus pitch bend
comment 4906 times 2: phase increment
comment 4908 patch the sample loop
comment 490B advance the bend
comment 4911 voice 2, the same without bend
comment 4917 U = voice 2 wave
comment 4929 patch the sample loop
comment 492C voice 3
comment 4932 S = voice 3 wave
comment 4945 patch the sample loop
comment 4948 DP $49: the loop patches itself
comment 494C with 2-byte direct stores
comment 494F 227 samples per pass

label 4954 SAMPLOOP
block 4954 SAMPLOOP: 77 cycles per sample. Each voice's 16-bit phase lives in
block 4954 the operand of its LDD: add the frequency, store the result back
block 4954 into the LDD, then index the wave with the phase high byte. A is a
block 4954 signed index, which is why wave pointers aim at the middle of each
block 4954 table. Voice 1 and 2 samples are stored into the two ADDB operands,
block 4954 so the three are summed and sent to the DAC with no extra loads.
comment 4954 voice 1 phase
comment 4957 + frequency (from MIXER)
comment 495A store the new phase
comment 495C sample = wave[phase high]
comment 495E into the first ADDB
comment 4960 voice 2 phase
comment 4963 + frequency
comment 4966 store the new phase
comment 4968 sample
comment 496A into the second ADDB
comment 496C voice 3 phase
comment 496F + frequency
comment 4972 store the new phase
comment 4974 voice 3 sample
comment 4976 + voice 1 sample
comment 4978 + voice 2 sample
comment 497A out to the DAC
comment 4981 back to DP $48
comment 4985 restore S

# ------------------------------------------------------------- sequencer
label 4989 HOLD
block 4989 HOLD: count down a sounding note. While it lasts, loop the envelope
block 4989 over frames 3-11. When it ends, play the release frames, or stay
block 4989 in the sustain if the note is tied to the next one.
comment 4989 (loaded but not used)
comment 498C one tick less
comment 4990 reached frame 12?
comment 4996 loop back to frame 3

label 499B RELEASE
comment 499B tied (no rest after it)?
comment 499F tied: stay in sustain
comment 49A3 release: frames 12-14

label 49A8 REST
block 49A8 REST: count down a rest, playing instrument 0 (silence).
comment 49AB instrument 0

label 49B3 SEQUENCER
block 49B3 SEQUENCER: one tick of the song for each voice. U = voice record.

label 49C2 SEQVOICE
block 49C2 SEQVOICE: fetch a pattern if the voice has none, then count down
block 49C2 the current note or rest, or else run pattern commands.
comment 49C2 current pattern, 0 if none

label 49C6 NEXTPAT
comment 49C6 read the song list
comment 49C8 pattern number
comment 49CA 0: voice finished
comment 49D0 $FF: restart the song

label 49D2 VOICEDONE
comment 49D2 stay at frame 0; the song list is not advanced

comment 49D5 save the song pointer
comment 49D7 look the pattern up
comment 49E0 note still sounding?
comment 49E4 rest still running?
comment 49EA command byte
comment 49EE index the BRA table
comment 49F3 handler; X is past the command

label 49F7 CMDTABLE
block 49F7 CMDTABLE: pattern commands. Command byte n jumps via entry n.
block 49F7   0                  end of pattern: fetch the next from the song list
block 49F7   1                  no operation
block 49F7   2 i (w p) x 15     define instrument i: 15 frames of wave, pitch
block 49F7   3 n d r            note n: sounds d ticks, then r ticks of release
block 49F7                      and rest. r = 0 ties it to the next note, which
block 49F7                      then continues the envelope instead of restarting.
block 49F7   4, 5, 6 i          select instrument i for the following notes
block 49F7   7 x hh ll          set the pitch bend (x ignored)
block 49F7   8 x hh ll          set the bend rate (x ignored)
block 49F7 Only voice 1 applies bend. Command 3 ends the voice's tick; all the
block 49F7 others run the next command straight away.
comment 49F7 0: end of pattern
comment 49F9 1: no operation
comment 49FB 2: define instrument
comment 49FD 3: note
comment 49FF 4: select instrument
comment 4A01 5: select instrument
comment 4A03 6: select instrument
comment 4A05 7: set pitch bend
comment 4A07 8: set bend rate

label 4A09 CMDNEXT
block 4A09 CMDNEXT: save the pattern pointer and run the next command now.
label 4A0D C_ENDPAT
label 4A0F C_NOP
comment 4A0F (A is not used)

label 4A13 C_DEFINS
block 4A13 C_DEFINS: command 2. Builds instrument i at INSTRS + 60*i. Waves
block 4A13 16-23 (noise) are pitched down 40 notes.
comment 4A15 60 bytes per instrument
comment 4A1F 15 frames
comment 4A23 A = wave, B = pitch offset
comment 4A25 noise wave?
comment 4A29 pitch it down 40 notes
comment 4A2C look up the wave pointer
comment 4A31 frame +0: wave pointer
comment 4A33 frame +2: pitch times 2
comment 4A34 (+3 is skipped)

label 4A3E C_NOTE
block 4A3E C_NOTE: command 3 n d r.
comment 4A3E A = note, B = duration
comment 4A41 V_NOTE: note times 2
comment 4A44 V_DUR = d - 1
comment 4A46 previous note had a rest?
comment 4A4A yes: restart the envelope
comment 4A4D (branches to the next line)
comment 4A4F A = rest length
comment 4A55 save the pattern pointer
comment 4A57 sound the selected instrument
comment 4A5B with a rest: V_GAP = r - 1,
comment 4A62 V_DUR = d

label 4A65 C_SETINS
block 4A65 C_SETINS: commands 4, 5 and 6. Select instrument i.
label 4A71 C_BEND
block 4A71 C_BEND: command 7. Set the pitch bend.
comment 4A71 skip the ignored byte
label 4A79 C_BRATE
block 4A79 C_BRATE: command 8. Set the bend rate.
comment 4A79 skip the ignored byte

# ------------------------------------------------------------- PWM
label 4A81 PWMSTEP
block 4A81 PWMSTEP: pulse-width modulation of wave 13 (WPWM). Moves the edge
block 4A81 at the end of the high part 4 samples per call, sweeping it back and
block 4A81 forth over the second half of the table. The values written are
block 4A81 already biased: $4E is the high level, $06 the low level.
block 4A81 WPWM2 and WPWM4 are copies made at startup and do not sweep.
comment 4A81 X = edge
comment 4A87 direction
comment 4A8B shrink: write low, backwards
comment 4A99 back to the middle?
comment 4A9D turn round
label 4AA3 PWMGROW
comment 4AA3 grow: write high, forwards
comment 4AB1 wrapped past the end?
comment 4AB3 turn round

# ------------------------------------------------------------- wave tables
label 4ABC MAKEWAVES
block 4ABC MAKEWAVES: build the 256-byte signed wave tables. Full amplitude
block 4ABC is about +/-42 ($24/$DC for the pulses). Half and quarter
block 4ABC amplitude copies give the volume steps. Finally $2A is added to
block 4ABC every byte, so the sum of three voices centres on the DAC's middle.
comment 4ABC WPULSE: 224 high
comment 4AC8 then 32 low
comment 4AD1 WPWM: 88 low
comment 4ADD then 168 high
comment 4AE6 WOFF: silence
comment 4AF2 WSINE: second half is
comment 4AF5 the first half negated
comment 4B00 half and quarter copies
comment 4B64 bias every table, noise too

label 4B73 HALVE
block 4B73 HALVE: Y[i] = X[i] / 2 (signed) for 256 bytes.

label 4B7D WAVEPTRS
block 4B7D WAVEPTRS: wave number to table, used by command 2.
block 4B7D   0 silence, 1-4 sine/saw/triangle/pulse, 5-8 half and 9-12 quarter
block 4B7D   amplitude of those, 13-15 PWM full/half/quarter, 16-23 noise.

# ------------------------------------------------------------- song data
label 532D FREQTAB
block 532D FREQTAB: 128 notes, each half its phase increment (MIXER doubles
block 532D it). Indexed by (note + frame pitch offset) times 2. Successive
block 532D entries step by a semitone; note 60 is roughly middle C.

label 542D SONG1
label 5478 SONG2
label 54C1 SONG3
block 542D SONG1, SONG2, SONG3: song lists for voices 1-3.
block 5478 Voice 2's list stops the voice with 0; the bytes after it are unused.
block 54C1 Voice 3's list has no 0 or $FF terminator; it runs into PATTERNS.

label 553B PATTERNS
block 553B PATTERNS: pattern number to address. Entry 0 is unused, because a 0
block 553B in a song list stops the voice.

block 55A1 Patterns. Pattern 1 begins by defining instrument 0 as 15 silent
block 55A1 frames, then defines the song's other instruments.

block 6395 WNOISE: eight 256-byte noise tables (waves 16-23).
label 6B95 WSINE
block 6B95 WSINE: first half of a sine. MAKEWAVES builds the second half
block 6B95 at $6C15.
label 6C95 WSAW
block 6C95 WSAW: sawtooth.
label 6D95 WTRI
block 6D95 WTRI: triangle.

